  "body": "Part of the worlds design program: [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955). Statements S30–S35 of the 2026-11-04 brief.\n\n## The model (Drew, in substance)\n\n- **Libraries themselves are pure.** The core app passes the platform in — or specific functions from the platform.\n- An app can thereby **ask what actions some entity should perform**, automatically or in response — effects are requested through the passed-in surface, never reached around it.\n- The security property: **an app only has access to the world-level capabilities that are passed to it.** Nothing ambient.\n- Convention: access is written as **`context.platform.<function name>`**, and **their code won't compile if it doesn't exist** — absence is a compile error, not a runtime surprise.\n- A **context object** was the recommendation that landed in technical discussions Drew saw — but this design must check whether there is a **better best practice** rather than adopting it by default.\n- Because this is a peer language, some worlds may **accept specific functions from users**; they can **trust exactly what a function would do** — function-passing is part of the same model, not an exception to it (this is the seed of the dynamic-user-functions design).\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Context object vs explicit function parameters vs a capabilities record: ergonomics in Roc specifically (the existing example protocol in `pkgs/id/examples/roc-world/RocCap.roc` is data point #1 — pure helpers, app owns its model).\n- Granularity of what's passed: whole platform, per-domain sub-records (fs, net, time), or individual functions? Passing narrower than the platform is the \"asks vs uses\" story at the source-code level.\n- How does the passed surface appear in a library's type signature so the import checker (library-imports design child) can verify subset-ness mechanically?\n- Versioning: when the platform grows a function, do contexts gain it silently (capability expansion — see the change-notices child) or is the context surface pinned per world?\n\n## Related\n\n- Core/app platform design: [#2028](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/2028)\n- Capability-model and library-imports design children of [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955)\n- OCAP prior-art research child of [#5955](https://github.com/developing-today/code/issues/5955) (this model *is* object-capability discipline — the research should say where we follow it and where we deliberately don't)\n\n## Done looks like\n\nA design doc naming the chosen passing mechanism (with the alternatives' rejection reasons), the Roc-level conventions, and how library signatures expose their platform needs.\n",
